(\r\n    exists().where(Child.parent_id == Parent.id)\r\n)\r\n```\r\n\r\nSome code which creates a child:\r\n```python\r\nsession.add(Child(parent_id=parent.id, ...))\r\n# session.flush()\r\nsession.expire(parent, ['has_child'])\r\n```\r\n\r\nSome code which checks whether the parent has a child:\r\n```python\r\nif parent.has_child:\r\n    ...\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese latter two snippets run one after the other in the same session. The session is using standard settings with auto-flush on and auto-commit off.\r\n\r\nThe problem is that the last snippet observes `parent.has_child == False`, unless the `session.flush()` line from the snippet before is uncommented. I would expect that accessing `has_child` after it has been expired would cause a query to be issued to the database, and this query would cause the session to be flushed so that the child would be visible to the query. However, this does not happen, and somehow the column property still sees no child.\r\n\r\nEven more strangely, this only seems to happen if `parent.has_child` was *not* accessed before the child was added.","closed_by":{"login":"sqlalchemy-bot","id":36047385,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2MDQ3Mzg1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/36047385?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy-bot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://a